PCI: dwc: a host whose endpoint is powered later may skip the link wait

dw_pcie_host_init() waits for the link (10 x 90 ms) whatever the outcome, and ignores the result. On the
UMS9620 the PCIe root port serves the Marlin3 (SC2355) Wi-Fi/BT chip, which is off until its own driver
(wcn_bsp, loaded from userspace) powers it and brings the link up through sprd_pcie_configure_device(). At
probe there is nothing to train with: "Phy link never came up" after 0.9 s, every boot, with the kernel's
init thread waiting (pcie-sprd's own check after it cost another 0.9 s). A controller driver that knows sets
skip_link_wait; the header defines DW_PCIE_HAS_SKIP_LINK_WAIT so that a driver shared with kernels without
this patch can test for it. 6.18 only until it is checked against 7.x.

--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware.h
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware.h
@@ -437,6 +437,9 @@
 	bool			ecam_enabled;
 	bool			native_ecam;
 	bool                    skip_l23_ready;
+	/* the endpoint is powered later by its own driver: no link to wait for at probe */
+	bool			skip_link_wait;
+#define DW_PCIE_HAS_SKIP_LINK_WAIT
 };
 
 struct dw_pcie_ep_ops {
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
@@ -665,7 +665,8 @@
 	}
 
 	/* Ignore errors, the link may come up later */
-	dw_pcie_wait_for_link(pci);
+	if (!pp->skip_link_wait)
+		dw_pcie_wait_for_link(pci);
 
 	ret = pci_host_probe(bridge);
 	if (ret)
